    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's an interesting way to put it. You mentioned \"oppressor-core\" critiques—it feels like a lot of people are arguing that when art becomes just a tool for these kinds of social critiques, it loses the ability to be actually \"good\" or transcendent, and just becomes propaganda. Do you think there's a legitimate loss of artistic quality when the message becomes the only thing that matters?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The tension you identify between social utility and aesthetic transcendence is a central debate in postmodern theory, where some argue that fetishizing messaging can indeed overlay art with didactic constraints. Others would contend that the \"loss\" of traditional beauty is merely a transition to evaluating success through the lens of cultural relevance rather than formal harmony, a shift already evident in modern archaeology which prioritizes functional understanding over static admiration. Ultimately, the value of such work often lies not in diminishing beauty, but in expanding the definition of what art can achieve when it refuses to remain merely decorative.",
      "source": "target",
